WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.883
United States: federal preemption pressure, state frontier laws.

2
00:00:04.163 --> 00:00:05.315
Federal policy.

3
00:00:05.595 --> 00:00:15.579
The administration’s AI Action Plan (July 2025) set a policy of accelerating AI development with minimal regulatory burden.

4
00:00:15.859 --> 00:00:28.616
Executive Order 14365, “Ensuring a National Policy Framework for Artificial Intelligence” (11 December 2025), went further against state rules.

5
00:00:28.896 --> 00:00:47.477
It directed the Attorney General to create an AI Litigation Task Force to challenge state AI laws inconsistent with the federal policy; told the Commerce Department to evaluate state laws and identify “onerous” ones; tied some federal broadband funding to states’ AI laws;

6
00:00:47.757 --> 00:01:01.944
asked the FTC and FCC for policy actions; and called for a legislative recommendation for a federal framework that would preempt conflicting state laws, while preserving state authority in areas such as child safety.

7
00:01:02.224 --> 00:01:15.835
An executive order cannot by itself preempt state law, and as of November 2026 there is no federal AI statute of general application, so the state laws below are in force or scheduled.

8
00:01:16.115 --> 00:01:31.496
California SB 53, the Transparency in Frontier Artificial Intelligence Act, signed on 29 September 2025 and in effect from 1 January 2026, applies to large frontier developers.

9
00:01:31.776 --> 00:01:47.285
It requires them to publish a framework on their websites describing how they incorporate national and international standards and industry best practices, creates a channel for reporting critical safety incidents to the state’s Office of Emergency Services,

10
00:01:47.565 --> 00:01:52.301
protects whistleblowers, and is enforced by the Attorney General with civil penalties.

11
00:01:52.581 --> 00:02:00.645
New York’s RAISE Act was signed on 19 December 2025 and amended on 27 March 2026.

12
00:02:00.925 --> 00:02:20.637
As summarized by counsel, the amended law takes effect on 1 January 2027, covers developers of models trained with more than 10 to the 26 operations that have over 500 million dollars in annual revenue, requires a published frontier AI framework and transparency reports,

13
00:02:20.917 --> 00:02:34.720
and requires reporting critical safety incidents within 72 hours (24 hours when there is imminent risk of death or serious injury), with oversight by a new office in the Department of Financial Services.

14
00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:48.248
The labs’ safety frameworks from lesson 7 are, in part, how they comply with these laws; OpenAI’s May 2026 Frontier Governance Framework explicitly maps its practices onto them.
